{
 "id": "ep014",
 "title": "Why Is the Fed About to Raise Rates Again?",
 "voice": "am_michael",
 "speed": 1.0,
 "description": "The Federal Reserve meets on Wednesday, October 28, 2026, with US inflation at 3.7% (September CPI) and the fed funds rate at 3.75%-4% after September's first hike in more than three years. Markets price only about an 8% chance of a hike this week but about 84% before year-end, with December 9 the date to watch. We explain how the Fed's 'thermostat' works, why energy prices after the Iran war pushed inflation up, the argument inside the Fed (prices vs. jobs: September added just 29,000 jobs), what it means for mortgages (7.32% this week), credit cards and savers, and why consumer sentiment (48.0) is near its lowest in 74 years.\n\nDave and Linda are made-up characters. Real officials are shown as generic labelled stick figures; quotes are attributed as reported. Not financial advice. We don't take sides: we just count the facts.\n\nSources:\nForbes, What To Look For From The Fed's October Meeting (Oct 19, 2026): https://www.forbes.com/sites/simonmoore/2026/10/19/what-to-look-for-from-the-feds-october-meeting/\nJ.P. Morgan / Chase, Will the Fed hike rates in October?: https://www.chase.com/personal/investments/learning-and-insights/article/will-the-federal-reserve-hike-rates-at-october-2026-fomc-meeting\nCharles Schwab, market update (Oct 23, 2026): https://www.schwab.com/learn/story/stock-market-update-open\nABC News, Consumer sentiment nears all-time low ahead of midterms: https://abcnews.com/Business/consumer-sentiment-nears-time-low-ahead-midterms/story?id=137507935\nAl Jazeera, US consumer sentiment hits a near-record low (Oct 23, 2026): https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/10/23/us-consumer-sentiment-hits-a-near-record-low-before-midterm-elections\nFreddie Mac, Mortgage Rates Decrease to 7.32% (Oct 22, 2026): https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/10/22/3386709/0/en/mortgage-rates-decrease-to-7-32.html\n\n#federalreserve #interestrates #inflation #mortgage #explained #stickfigure #news",
